    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **No, you should not make an issue of the panel’s gender composition, nor is it on its own a reliable sign of bias.** \n\nMaking an accusation or formal issue based solely on the panel's gender is far more likely to damage your professional reputation than to yield any positive outcome. Here is a breakdown of why, along with an objective look at the hiring factors and how you should handle the situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does an all-female panel indicate bias?\n* **Statistical reality vs. intent:** Just as an all-male panel in engineering does not automatically prove discrimination against female applicants, an all-female panel does not prove bias against male applicants. Panels are usually assembled based on role relevance (e.g., HR lead, department director, client rep, technical director) rather than demographic engineering.\n* **Lack of actionable evidence:** To substantiate discrimination or unfair hiring practices, there must be demonstrable procedural irregularities, discriminatory statements, or clear deviations from documented scoring criteria. Panel composition alone does not meet that standard.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Understanding the Hiring Decision\nWhile you have more years of experience and have led three major projects, \"Head of Group\" roles at engineering consultancies are typically evaluated on criteria that extend beyond technical seniority:\n\n* **Role requirements (Technical vs. Commercial/Leadership):** At 8 years and with a chartered licence, the successful candidate already meets the senior threshold. For group head positions, consultancies often prioritize:\n  * **Business development and client retention** (the announcement specifically highlighted her *\"excellent client relationships\"*).\n  * **People management, culture, and team retention.**\n  * **Strategic vision for group growth.**\n* **Interview performance:** Once a shortlist of three is established, paper qualifications (degrees, years on paper) matter much less. Final decisions usually come down to interview performance, responses to situational leadership scenarios, and how well each candidate presented their vision for the team.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay in Feedback\nA two-week wait for written feedback is standard in professional services:\n* Offers and contracts are often still being finalized.\n* Drafting formal interview feedback requires HR review and sign-off, which often gets deprioritized relative to urgent project delivery.\n* Many organizations do not provide detailed written feedback by default, or take several weeks to compile it.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite, professional follow-up:**  \n   Frame your request around personal development rather than questioning the outcome.\n   > *\"Dear [HR Contact/Hiring Lead], I hope you are having a productive week. Following up on my interview for the Head of Structural Engineering role, I would greatly appreciate any brief notes or feedback the panel might have regarding areas where I can strengthen my profile for future leadership roles. Thank you again for your time and consideration.\"*\n\n2. **Reflect on the interview focus:**  \n   Assess whether your interview discussions leaned heavily toward technical delivery/past projects, or if they equally addressed winning work, managing clients, and financial group performance.\n\n3. **Protect your industry standing:**  \n   The engineering consultancy world is relatively small. Maintaining a gracious, professional relationship with this firm leaves the door open for future senior openings or collaboration, whereas challenging the panel's gender composition without evidence will close doors quickly.\n",
